# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <croi/syscall.h>

/* CROI_SYS_IOPORTS_REQUEST */
int64_t ioports_request(uint32_t ioport, uint64_t io_addr, uint64_t len);
/* CROI_SYS_IOPORTS_RELEASE */
int64_t ioports_release(uint32_t ioport, uint64_t io_addr, uint64_t len);
```

# DESCRIPTION

On amd64, `ioports_request` lets the calling process execute `in` and `out` on the <len> ports from <io_addr>, and `ioports_release` takes that back.
<ioport> is a resource(2) of kind IOPORT that grants the range.
Each process has a permission bitmap; a port the process has not asked for faults.
A change reaches every CPU before the call returns.
The ports are 0 to 0xFFFF; <len> must not be 0 and the range must lie inside them (`CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`).
Releasing ports never requested is not an error.

On arm64 and rv64 the calls are `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED`.

